Abstract = {On the island of Guahan (Guam) native and endemic species are an
   integral part of the CHamoru language, rituals, and practices. They are
   interconnected with their culture and honored together with the ocean,
   the land, and their ancestors. Presently, this spiritual connection is
   being jeopardized as military expansion threatens the sacred lands of
   Tailalo' and Litekyan. Phylogenetic and ethnographic research, connected
   to the principles of environmental justice, has brought attention to the
   social movement to protect the last hayun lagu tree (Serianthes
   nelsonii) at Tailalo'. This opinion piece is a rallying cry to safeguard
   the last hayun lagu and to confront the U.S. National Environmental
   Policy Act and the U.S. Endangered Species Act, as its regulations do
   not mirror the island's social and cultural system and fail to
   incorporate Indigenous and scientific perspectives to protect Indigenous
   biocultural heritage.},
