Volume = {6},
Number = {1},
Pages = {58-65},
Month = {FEB},
Abstract = {Biodiversity conservation requires prioritization to be effective.
   Biodiversity hotspots and conservation planning identify where to focus
   conservation efforts, but it is unclear when conservation is most
   successful. Our goals were to: (a) investigate if hot moments for
   conservation occur, (b) calculate how important and prevalent they are,
   and (c) discuss what may catalyze hot moments for conservation. We
   analyzed the worldwide network of protected areas since inception,
   analyzing both all countries, and those 35 countries that contained at
   least 1\% of either the total count or the total area protected
   globally. The evidence for hot moments for conservation was very strong.
   Among all countries, 44\% protected more than half of their protected
   area in 1 year, and 61\% did so in one 5-year period. The 35 countries
   that contain most of the protected area globally (77\%) protected 23\%
   and 49\%, respectively, within 1 or 5 years. Hot moments often coincided
   with societal upheaval such as the collapse of the USSR or the end of
   colonialism. Conservationists need to account for hot moments for
   conservation to be most effective.},
